1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Nature's precious elixir.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
So powerful, it can carve our landscape.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Yet so nurturing, it can spawn life.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:14,000
We bottle its goodness,

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:16,000
showcase its beauty,

6
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
and seek its wonders at the far edge of our solar system.

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, Water on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Don't let this deceptively simple substance fool you.

9
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:49,200
Beyond water's common veneer lies tantalizing complexity.

10
00:00:49,201 --> 00:00:54,460
Beneath the surface lurks a dynamic entity.

11
00:00:54,461 --> 00:00:57,040
Water radiates with the essence of life.

12
00:00:57,041 --> 00:01:04,040
Yet, it often unleashes its savage ability to destroy.

13
00:01:04,041 --> 00:01:16,040
Gushing from great heights, it packs the power to light up our cities.

14
00:01:16,041 --> 00:01:18,040
Trickling is a single drop.

15
00:01:18,041 --> 00:01:21,040
It conveys our humanity.

16
00:01:21,041 --> 00:01:24,040
Water is the very stuff that we are made of.

17
00:01:24,041 --> 00:01:27,580
more than 70% of your body is water.

18
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:34,240
Water is the necessary highway that gets all the chemistry going that is your metabolism.

19
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:40,280
Within Earth's 6 billion people flows 4 billion gallons of water,

20
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:44,960
almost as much as pours over Niagara Falls in one hour.

21
00:01:48,620 --> 00:01:51,860
The world surrounding us is no less awash in water.

22
00:01:54,040 --> 00:02:03,040
It blankets 70% of the Earth's surface, a total of 326 million cubic miles.

23
00:02:03,041 --> 00:02:08,040
But of that staggering figure, only 2.5% is freshwater.

24
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:15,040
And most of that amount is locked away in ice caps and glaciers.

25
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:20,040
Just 1% remains to sustain us all.

26
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:34,480
Nature magnifies this sobering reality, distributing our precious supply unevenly, both geographically

27
00:02:34,481 --> 00:02:37,340
and over time.

28
00:02:37,341 --> 00:02:42,360
The result is that more than a billion people lack access to clean drinking water.

29
00:02:42,361 --> 00:02:45,640
Without water, we're dead.

30
00:02:45,641 --> 00:02:48,140
It's a very undervalued commodity.

31
00:02:48,141 --> 00:02:51,480
And we only know that its value is there when we don't have it.

32
00:02:51,481 --> 00:02:55,260
I think Benjamin Franklin said we only know the worth of water when the well is dry.

33
00:02:57,220 --> 00:03:02,340
Where water is plentiful, however, it's risen to new heights as a slickly marketed product.

34
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:07,920
Media stories of tap water laced with pollutants and impurities

35
00:03:07,921 --> 00:03:12,820
have convinced millions that the healthier, better tasting choice comes in a bottle.

36
00:03:15,180 --> 00:03:19,060
25% of America's brands are purified municipal waters.

37
00:03:19,061 --> 00:03:23,160
The majority are drawn from natural springs.

38
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:28,920
What precisely is a spring?

39
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:39,180
Camp Holly Springs in Richmond, Virginia, serving the region since 1923, is an ideal place to clue you in.

40
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:45,580
This is the surface expression of Camp Holly Springs.

41
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:51,400
This is very similar to probably 99% of the springs that people actually see.

42
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:57,640
What happens is that precipitation, rainfall, is falling to the highlands behind us.

43
00:03:57,641 --> 00:04:02,480
It moves through the soil into the underlying aquifer and becomes groundwater.

44
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:06,500
Now, two, three years later, it is discharged.

45
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:13,260
The crucial spot where the water actually breaks through the surface lies farther uphill.

46
00:04:15,580 --> 00:04:19,360
sealed in an enclosure called the spring house.

47
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:24,440
The main purpose between the spring house and containing the spring

48
00:04:24,441 --> 00:04:30,780
is to keep any sort of surface contaminants out of the spring, the groundwater itself.

49
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:34,840
It keeps surface water out through rain or runoff.

50
00:04:34,841 --> 00:04:38,640
It keeps people from being able to introduce contaminants

51
00:04:38,641 --> 00:04:44,300
because really the water never sees the light of day before it's put into that bottle.

52
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:50,580
Camp Holly's owners don't pump the water directly from the aquifer.

53
00:04:50,581 --> 00:04:56,580
Gravity carries the water from the seepage point, through a pipe, to a pump house.

54
00:04:56,581 --> 00:05:01,580
Such a method avoids any possibility of disturbing the spring's natural flow.

55
00:05:01,581 --> 00:05:06,580
The water enters through this pipe and flows under its own power to our holding tank,

56
00:05:06,581 --> 00:05:09,580
which has a capacity of 500 gallons.

57
00:05:09,581 --> 00:05:12,580
From the holding tank, we pump through a quarter-mile pipeline

58
00:05:12,581 --> 00:05:17,140
pipeline to our production facility using a seven and a half horsepower pump.

59
00:05:17,141 --> 00:05:21,520
When we're not pumping to the production facility, you can see the overflow pipe

60
00:05:21,521 --> 00:05:27,580
and the natural flow of the spring. At this point we have pristine water with

61
00:05:27,581 --> 00:05:32,140
32 parts per million total dissolved solids.

62
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:38,200
Outside the company's production facility, the spring water collects in a

63
00:05:38,201 --> 00:05:44,080
fourteen thousand gallon tank. From there, pumps convey it to the top of a 47-foot

64
00:05:44,081 --> 00:05:49,960
tower to begin a sterilization process using ozone.

65
00:05:49,961 --> 00:05:57,160
Ozone, an unstable form of oxygen, is a powerful oxidizing agent that destroys any

66
00:05:57,161 --> 00:06:03,340
microorganism it may contact. As the water descends from the top of the ozone

67
00:06:03,341 --> 00:06:08,832
mixing column, we inject a mixture of ozone gas and water at the base of the column.

68
00:06:08,833 --> 00:06:09,533
The ozone

69
00:06:09,534 --> 00:06:13,719
being a gas, it tries to ascend the column as the water is descending.

70
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:15,500
This creates a mixture of the

71
00:06:15,501 --> 00:06:20,631
two for contact time of over nine minutes. The ozone sterilizes the water.

72
00:06:20,632 --> 00:06:21,740
From this point,

73
00:06:21,741 --> 00:06:25,340
the water enters the production facility into our filler to fill bottles.

74
00:06:25,341 --> 00:06:36,700
part of bottled spring water's allure is that no two springs or the waters drawn from them

75
00:06:36,701 --> 00:06:43,800
are exactly alike a spring with an entirely different surface expression than camp hollies

76
00:06:43,801 --> 00:06:46,300
lies in picturesque iceland

77
00:06:46,301 --> 00:06:53,900
The water in this pristine lake

78
00:06:53,901 --> 00:06:55,860
percolates up from an aquifer

79
00:06:55,861 --> 00:06:57,440
reaching depths of a half mile

80
00:06:57,441 --> 00:07:02,080
The water begins as rain

81
00:07:02,081 --> 00:07:03,160
in the mountains of Iceland

82
00:07:03,161 --> 00:07:07,380
which trickles down beneath the ground

83
00:07:07,381 --> 00:07:09,100
into layers of volcanic rock

84
00:07:09,101 --> 00:07:13,660
This country Iceland is drifting apart

85
00:07:13,661 --> 00:07:18,060
by approximately two and a half centimeters, one inch a year.

86
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:22,880
And this gradually breaks up the lava layers,

87
00:07:22,881 --> 00:07:28,160
and this makes it possible for the water to travel through.

88
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:34,820
The lava rock acts as a natural filtration system for the water

89
00:07:34,821 --> 00:07:36,580
before it settles in the aquifer.

90
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:42,520
From there, pumps carry it to the production facility of Iceland Spring.

91
00:07:43,660 --> 00:07:46,100
which bottles more than two million gallons every year.

92
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:52,300
That's a modest amount compared to the output of major industry players like DS Waters.

93
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:57,880
It operates one of its 28 plants in Sacramento, California.

94
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:09,620
Every day, trucks complete a two-hour drive to deliver water pumped from a protected spring in the Sierra Nevada Mountains.

95
00:08:09,621 --> 00:08:19,160
The water, like that at Camp Holly Springs and Iceland Spring, undergoes thorough filtration

96
00:08:19,161 --> 00:08:24,240
and disinfection.

97
00:08:24,241 --> 00:08:27,900
But the plant's most intensive purification techniques are reserved for the second type

98
00:08:27,901 --> 00:08:36,140
of water processed here, Sacramento's municipal supply.

99
00:08:36,141 --> 00:08:43,140
journey is more rigorous, passing first through a phalanx of sand and charcoal filters.

100
00:08:46,020 --> 00:08:52,260
The most crucial filter of all lies further down the line, which uses a process called

101
00:08:52,261 --> 00:08:53,577
reverse osmosis.

102
00:08:53,578 --> 00:08:59,420
A pump forces the water under high pressure through a vessel encasing a

103
00:08:59,421 --> 00:09:00,911
semi-permeable membrane.

104
00:09:00,912 --> 00:09:05,320
The membrane consists of multiple layers that act as a very fine filter

105
00:09:05,321 --> 00:09:07,040
featuring molecular-sized pores.

106
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:09,940
The membrane traps contaminants and debris

107
00:09:09,941 --> 00:09:11,500
from the municipal water supply

108
00:09:11,501 --> 00:09:13,340
that are too large to penetrate.

109
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:18,680
But the water molecules are small enough

110
00:09:18,681 --> 00:09:22,300
to fit through and flow into the membrane's core.

111
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:27,520
The municipal water supply that we have in this location

112
00:09:27,521 --> 00:09:30,140
is 100 parts per million, total dissolved solids.

113
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:33,260
And through reverse osmosis,

114
00:09:33,261 --> 00:09:36,040
we're able to reduce that to one part per million.

115
00:09:37,660 --> 00:09:42,440
The step following reverse osmosis is to add minerals back in for taste.

116
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:47,240
Purified water can have a kind of a rough taste across your tongue.

117
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:53,080
And when we add minerals back in, we ensure that we have a pleasant tasting product for our customers.

118
00:09:54,520 --> 00:09:58,080
The water then undergoes two final disinfection processes.

119
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:02,100
First, it's bathed in ultraviolet light.

120
00:10:02,101 --> 00:10:05,020
and then subjected to an ozone treatment.

121
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:09,640
Finally, it's off to the filling station.

122
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:17,160
A large part of bottled water's appeal is that consumers deem it fashionable.

123
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:25,060
But eons ago, the basic idea sprang from a matter of sheer necessity.

124
00:10:26,120 --> 00:10:31,200
Early men realized that water was something we need, but it isn't always where we need it.

125
00:10:31,201 --> 00:10:33,740
so they learned quickly to be able to transport it,

126
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:37,960
and putting it into vessels was the easiest way to bring it to places where it didn't exist.

127
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:44,380
The first things that were used to bottle water were things like animal bladders and hollowed-out gourds.

128
00:10:44,381 --> 00:10:53,580
There are many people who think that ceramic pottery came initially from the need to store water reliably in arid situations.

129
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:59,840
Civilizations, including Greece and Rome, took water management a step further.

130
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:05,440
engineering monumental ways to transport water from distant rivers.

131
00:11:05,441 --> 00:11:10,800
The Roman aqueducts delivered millions of gallons of fresh water daily to the

132
00:11:10,801 --> 00:11:11,840
imperial capital.

133
00:11:11,841 --> 00:11:15,600
But delivering water was one thing.

134
00:11:15,601 --> 00:11:18,000
Keeping it clean was another.

135
00:11:18,001 --> 00:11:21,280
In the fifth century,

136
00:11:21,281 --> 00:11:26,360
after Rome's fall, innovators in Venice devised an ingenious way to filter rain

137
00:11:26,361 --> 00:11:28,160
water stored in the city's wells.

138
00:11:28,161 --> 00:11:37,480
Workers placed a perforated cistern in a conical hole about 10 feet deep.

139
00:11:37,481 --> 00:11:41,980
They filled the space surrounding it with fine sand.

140
00:11:41,981 --> 00:11:46,760
Sloped stone surfaces channeled the rainwater into the sand, where it flowed into the cistern

141
00:11:46,761 --> 00:11:50,180
through the holes near its base.

142
00:11:50,181 --> 00:11:55,460
Most of the water resided not in the cistern, but in the sand, which continuously trapped

143
00:11:55,461 --> 00:11:59,060
particle debris and microorganisms.

144
00:11:59,061 --> 00:12:03,580
Every time you withdrew water, it was effectively filtered through the sand.

145
00:12:03,581 --> 00:12:09,060
And that was the epitome of water treatment technology in Western Europe.

146
00:12:09,061 --> 00:12:15,420
But over the next millennia, the Venetians' example failed to catch on.

147
00:12:15,421 --> 00:12:21,860
And as Europe's population exploded, the quality of its water plummeted.

148
00:12:21,861 --> 00:12:26,920
In growing urban centers, waste and sewage fouled its taste and poisoned its purity.

149
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:31,300
It was very unfashionable to drink water.

150
00:12:32,140 --> 00:12:37,560
The only people who would drink water were people who had absolutely no other choice.

151
00:12:38,040 --> 00:12:40,220
What they would do is they drank beer.

152
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:43,080
And they drank what they called small beer,

153
00:12:43,081 --> 00:12:49,120
which was beer that had been watered down to an ethanol concentration of 1 to 2%.

154
00:12:49,121 --> 00:12:55,520
That was sufficient to depress the growth of fecal bacteria, which are dangerous to humans.

155
00:12:55,720 --> 00:12:58,380
But at the same time, it wouldn't dehydrate you.

156
00:12:59,400 --> 00:13:02,560
Europeans shunned water for the next 400 years.

157
00:13:05,100 --> 00:13:13,220
Then, in the 18th and 19th centuries, the rich rediscovered its healthful benefits in the pristine springs far from the cities.

158
00:13:15,020 --> 00:13:18,160
Water became not only acceptable, but cheap.

159
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:23,320
What ultimately happened was that people would know of a spring,

160
00:13:23,460 --> 00:13:25,840
and then they want to continue drinking that spring at home.

161
00:13:26,120 --> 00:13:28,280
And that really is how the bottled water business started.

162
00:13:29,300 --> 00:13:31,280
European water bottlers prospered.

163
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:35,080
The wave eventually hit America.

164
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:43,880
In 1855, New York's Saratoga Springs churned out 50 million bottles.

165
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:48,720
City dwellers weary of impure municipal water.

166
00:13:49,120 --> 00:13:53,860
plunked down the modern equivalent of more than $30 to purchase a single pint.

167
00:13:54,820 --> 00:14:00,140
By about 1900, bottled water industry in America was a big business.

168
00:14:00,900 --> 00:14:04,420
In 1913, something happened that changed all of that.

169
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:09,280
In Philadelphia, engineers who had been tinkering with this for years

170
00:14:09,281 --> 00:14:13,360
finally came up with a method of adding liquid chlorine to water,

171
00:14:13,720 --> 00:14:16,600
which would at least partially disinfect it

172
00:14:16,601 --> 00:14:19,800
and essentially make it entirely safe to drink.

173
00:14:20,100 --> 00:14:25,460
And so one of the very large reasons for buying bottled water disappeared overnight.

174
00:14:25,461 --> 00:14:30,300
The bottled water industry in America collapsed and almost disappeared altogether.

175
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:37,940
America's bottled brands confine themselves largely to office water coolers for the next six decades.

176
00:14:37,941 --> 00:14:46,280
But in the late 70s, slickly marketed brands from Europe revived America's love of the

177
00:14:46,281 --> 00:14:50,740
product, opening the floodgates for an industry now worth $10 billion.

178
00:14:50,741 --> 00:15:00,500
While many consumers embrace water as nature's ultimate beverage, it enjoys another identity

179
00:15:00,501 --> 00:15:03,040
as nature's definitive oddball.

180
00:15:03,041 --> 00:15:09,920
America's top-selling bottled waters are Pepsi's Aquafina and Coca-Cola's Dasani.

181
00:15:10,960 --> 00:15:15,960
Each is purified water drawn not from springs, but municipal water supplies.

182
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:20,760
Water will return on Modern Marvels.

183
00:15:22,700 --> 00:15:26,120
We now return to water on Modern Marvels.

184
00:15:29,580 --> 00:15:32,420
We think of water as an ordinary substance.

185
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:36,120
But it's anything but.

186
00:15:36,121 --> 00:15:40,580
The surprising truth is that this abundant compound doesn't behave the way most substances

187
00:15:40,581 --> 00:15:41,580
do.

188
00:15:41,581 --> 00:15:52,040
And its chemical and physical oddities are the key to its role as the matrix of life.

189
00:15:52,041 --> 00:15:57,660
We think nothing of the fact that ice floats on the surface of water.

190
00:15:57,661 --> 00:16:03,720
That happens because water expands when it freezes, increasing its volume by 9% and becoming

191
00:16:03,721 --> 00:16:05,820
less dense.

192
00:16:05,821 --> 00:16:11,560
This is just one of water's properties that sets it apart as an oddball.

193
00:16:11,561 --> 00:16:17,720
Most substances, unlike water, are much more dense in their solid form than in their liquid

194
00:16:17,721 --> 00:16:18,720
form.

195
00:16:18,721 --> 00:16:22,160
Glacial acetic acid is an example of that.

196
00:16:22,161 --> 00:16:29,160
Here we have a sample of its solid form, and in these beakers we have a sample of its liquid form.

197
00:16:29,161 --> 00:16:37,160
As you can see, solid glacial acetic acid acts very differently than water does.

198
00:16:39,160 --> 00:16:43,160
Yes, this is how so-called normal compounds behave.

199
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:50,160
And if water wasn't such a rebel, the consequences would be catastrophic.

200
00:16:50,161 --> 00:16:56,880
If water behaved like most substances, if frozen water were more dense than liquid water,

201
00:16:57,220 --> 00:17:02,640
then every year when a pond froze over, it wouldn't freeze over, it would freeze under.

202
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:08,380
And the ice would sink to the bottom of the pond and then build its way up from the bottom to the top

203
00:17:08,381 --> 00:17:10,040
instead of from the top to the bottom.

204
00:17:10,041 --> 00:17:16,060
All the life in that pond would die because it wouldn't have any liquid water available to it.

205
00:17:17,300 --> 00:17:18,940
Nothing could survive.

206
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:29,280
So the fact that ice is less dense than liquid water enables life to carry on even when temperatures shift and get cold during the winter.

207
00:17:30,200 --> 00:17:32,740
What's responsible for water's wackiness?

208
00:17:33,960 --> 00:17:37,420
At the molecular level, it's electrically lopsided.

209
00:17:38,040 --> 00:17:47,480
The H2O molecule looks like Mickey Mouse ears, with a slightly negative charge near the oxygen atom and a slightly positive charge near the hydrogen atoms.

210
00:17:47,481 --> 00:17:56,140
Hardly anything has this great big negatively charged atom with these two little tiny positively charged atoms.

211
00:17:56,141 --> 00:17:59,740
It's what we call polar. It aligns itself in certain ways.

212
00:18:00,440 --> 00:18:05,360
When two water molecules are going toward each other, they have to orient themselves.

213
00:18:05,760 --> 00:18:12,920
So the positive portion of one water molecule is pointing toward the negative portion of another water molecule.

214
00:18:12,921 --> 00:18:19,560
This attractive force between the two molecules is what we call the hydrogen bond.

215
00:18:19,940 --> 00:18:25,040
And it's the hydrogen bond that gives water all of its unusual properties.

216
00:18:26,520 --> 00:18:30,940
The hydrogen bond compels water molecules to grab onto one another,

217
00:18:31,560 --> 00:18:33,880
accounting for another of water's curious attributes.

218
00:18:36,200 --> 00:18:42,140
Steel is more dense than water, so we wouldn't expect anything made of steel to float.

219
00:18:42,141 --> 00:18:46,620
We would, in fact, expect it to sink, like this steel paperclip.

220
00:18:47,020 --> 00:18:55,740
However, if we very carefully take our paperclip and place it just on the surface of the water,

221
00:18:56,320 --> 00:18:58,380
we'll find that it will float.

222
00:18:59,060 --> 00:19:04,560
You'll notice the subtle indentations that the paperclip is making in the surface of the water.

223
00:19:04,561 --> 00:19:12,080
This is because water's high surface tension allows the surface to act as if it were a skin.

224
00:19:12,140 --> 00:19:18,320
We see water's high surface tension in action whenever we overfill a glass.

225
00:19:19,160 --> 00:19:25,120
The molecules cling so tenaciously to each other that the water can rise significantly over the brim.

226
00:19:27,460 --> 00:19:32,340
Water's surface tension explains how some insects can walk atop ponds and lakes.

227
00:19:33,340 --> 00:19:36,760
It gives raindrops such a thick skin that they fall like bullets.

228
00:19:38,500 --> 00:19:41,400
Whittling away mountains over geologic time.

229
00:19:42,140 --> 00:19:49,240
and it enhances water's ability to ascend through the capillary systems of plants and trees.

230
00:19:50,680 --> 00:19:55,200
If water didn't have the fabulous surface tension property that it has,

231
00:19:55,920 --> 00:19:59,280
trees would be little stubby things, plants would be shorter,

232
00:19:59,281 --> 00:20:02,740
the very surface of the earth would look completely different.

233
00:20:05,620 --> 00:20:08,340
Water's single most significant feature, perhaps,

234
00:20:08,341 --> 00:20:12,280
is that practically anything except oil dissolves in it.

235
00:20:14,240 --> 00:20:17,840
Biologists theorize that water's role as the universal solvent

236
00:20:17,841 --> 00:20:21,320
enabled life to begin in the watery cauldron of our oceans.

237
00:20:24,320 --> 00:20:26,660
If you think about what is required

238
00:20:26,661 --> 00:20:30,260
to get the complexity necessary to evolve into life,

239
00:20:30,600 --> 00:20:33,320
you have to think about how many tries you could have.

240
00:20:33,900 --> 00:20:37,180
Tries meaning what ways could you combine molecules

241
00:20:37,181 --> 00:20:43,300
or even atoms to make molecules that might yield more complexity and evolve into life.

242
00:20:43,720 --> 00:20:46,380
And water is a perfect solvent for that to happen.

243
00:20:47,040 --> 00:20:53,100
The water has its polarity, which can align the bits in different attempts to arrange them

244
00:20:53,101 --> 00:20:55,760
in such a way that they'll chemically react with one another.

245
00:20:56,320 --> 00:20:59,340
And you can do it in unlimited number of tries

246
00:20:59,341 --> 00:21:03,200
because it allows things to move around in all kinds of direction.

247
00:21:03,201 --> 00:21:09,200
Amidst the countless molecules of water making life itself possible,

248
00:21:09,201 --> 00:21:13,200
Rome's scattered misfits even more peculiar than the rest.

249
00:21:13,201 --> 00:21:21,200
One in every 6,600 water molecules has hydrogen atoms that are heavier than normal.

250
00:21:21,201 --> 00:21:28,200
In a normal water molecule, the hydrogen atom's nucleus consists of a single proton.

251
00:21:28,201 --> 00:21:35,200
The heavy hydrogen atom called deuterium also contains a neutron in its nucleus.

252
00:21:35,201 --> 00:21:42,200
The result is a compound called deuterium oxide, also known as heavy water.

253
00:21:42,201 --> 00:21:49,200
Heavy water has special properties tailor-made for nuclear power generation.

254
00:21:49,201 --> 00:21:57,200
It's enabled engineers to design reactors that create fission using natural instead of enriched uranium.

255
00:21:57,201 --> 00:22:06,520
uranium, which is both difficult and prohibitively expensive to produce.

256
00:22:06,521 --> 00:22:12,760
Canada's Bruce Power Facility, about 220 miles northwest of Toronto, extracts its heavy water

257
00:22:12,761 --> 00:22:15,400
from neighboring Lake Huron.

258
00:22:15,401 --> 00:22:22,720
2,000 tons of the lake's water yields only 2 ounces of deuterium oxide.

259
00:22:22,721 --> 00:22:28,960
The US reactors use light water, but they have to have enriched uranium to cause the

260
00:22:28,961 --> 00:22:29,960
fission.

261
00:22:29,961 --> 00:22:30,960
We've gone the other way.

262
00:22:30,961 --> 00:22:36,200
The Canadian design is the natural uranium with a heavy water moderator that allows the

263
00:22:36,201 --> 00:22:39,040
fission to happen.

264
00:22:39,041 --> 00:22:45,720
In the reactor, 68 tons of heavy water surrounds cylinders containing the natural uranium fuel.

265
00:22:45,721 --> 00:22:51,440
In conventional water, most of the neutrons emitting from the uranium would be absorbed

266
00:22:51,441 --> 00:22:53,980
by the water molecules.

267
00:22:53,981 --> 00:22:56,820
But the heavy water repels nearly all of them,

268
00:22:56,821 --> 00:23:00,180
making them ricochet between the water molecules.

269
00:23:00,181 --> 00:23:03,520
This slows the neutrons, redirects many of them

270
00:23:03,521 --> 00:23:07,220
back through the uranium, and enables the chain reaction

271
00:23:07,221 --> 00:23:09,680
to be sustained.

272
00:23:09,681 --> 00:23:12,380
And this is what releases the heat and causes the water

273
00:23:12,381 --> 00:23:15,900
to heat up, which we carry off to the boilers to make steam,

274
00:23:15,901 --> 00:23:18,040
which eventually turns the turbines to make electricity.

275
00:23:21,440 --> 00:23:28,520
Heavy water may be a misfit plucked from an oddball substance, but without it, millions

276
00:23:28,521 --> 00:23:32,800
of Canadians would be totally in the dark.

277
00:23:32,801 --> 00:23:40,780
As it continues to energize a nation, water's standard variety endures as the lifeblood of

278
00:23:40,781 --> 00:23:44,440
the world's agriculture.

279
00:23:44,441 --> 00:23:52,120
The speed of sound and water is roughly 4500 feet per second, five times faster than its

280
00:23:52,121 --> 00:23:57,760
speed in the air, helping enable whales to communicate over distances of hundreds of

281
00:23:57,761 --> 00:23:59,440
miles.

