284
00:24:14,001 --> 00:24:19,000
Welcome to Nebraska, the heart of America's breadbasket.

285
00:24:19,001 --> 00:24:26,080
Here, farmers rely on a bounty of water to feed their thirsty crops, and in turn, a hungry

286
00:24:26,081 --> 00:24:30,900
world.

287
00:24:30,901 --> 00:24:35,780
Agriculture on this scale demands a larger, more consistently available volume of water

288
00:24:35,781 --> 00:24:39,560
than the unpredictable rains can deliver.

289
00:24:39,561 --> 00:24:46,560
where the state's growers sustain their fields using the time-honored art of irrigation.

290
00:24:47,700 --> 00:24:53,080
Farmers irrigate 50 million acres of farmland in the United States, swallowing up nearly

291
00:24:53,081 --> 00:24:55,314
40 percent of America's water.

292
00:24:55,315 --> 00:25:00,080
And in Nebraska, the principal source of that water lies trapped

293
00:25:01,560 --> 00:25:03,645
beneath the Earth's surface.

294
00:25:03,646 --> 00:25:08,560
A vast subterranean reservoir called the Ogallala Abbey is a large

295
00:25:09,560 --> 00:25:14,560
The aquifer stretches from South Dakota to New Mexico.

296
00:25:14,561 --> 00:25:18,560
Over the eons, rainwater has percolated downward,

297
00:25:18,561 --> 00:25:21,560
saturating the spaces between soil and rocks,

298
00:25:21,561 --> 00:25:26,560
and penetrating porous formations of sand, silt, and clay.

299
00:25:26,561 --> 00:25:31,560
Like a titanic sponge, the aquifer holds nearly a quadrillion gallons of water,

300
00:25:31,561 --> 00:25:38,560
enough to cover the continental United States to a depth of 16 inches.

301
00:25:38,561 --> 00:25:42,440
Every year, farmers pump about five trillion gallons from wells

302
00:25:42,441 --> 00:25:45,860
to irrigate what nature by herself could never sustain.

303
00:25:48,300 --> 00:25:52,420
These monstrous watering wands on wheels extending from the wells

304
00:25:52,421 --> 00:25:55,260
have become signature features of the Great Plains.

305
00:25:56,360 --> 00:25:59,380
They're aptly called center pivots.

306
00:26:00,280 --> 00:26:06,300
As the name would apply, a center pivot, it has a fixed point that it rotates around.

307
00:26:06,301 --> 00:26:12,180
and from the air it gives you a circular pattern that people may have seen when

308
00:26:12,181 --> 00:26:16,060
they're flying across the United States or even other parts of the world.

309
00:26:16,061 --> 00:26:29,100
It goes around typically in a clockwise direction, working just like a watch hand and going around

310
00:26:29,101 --> 00:26:35,880
around very slowly and applying the water evenly over the entire field as it moves.

311
00:26:35,881 --> 00:26:42,440
The machine itself is powered by small electric motors at each drive unit or each tower.

312
00:26:42,441 --> 00:26:46,220
They consume less than one horsepower each.

313
00:26:46,221 --> 00:26:53,460
A center pivot covering 130 acres typically takes three days to complete one rotation.

314
00:26:53,461 --> 00:26:57,060
can sprinkle a thousand gallons every minute,

315
00:26:57,061 --> 00:27:03,760
enough to fill an Olympic-sized pool in one hour.

316
00:27:03,761 --> 00:27:07,460
This model watering a sod farm outside of Omaha

317
00:27:07,461 --> 00:27:13,220
epitomizes a crucial element of every Center Pivot's engineering,

318
00:27:13,221 --> 00:27:19,120
keeping the linked spans of its lengthy pipeline properly aligned as they move.

319
00:27:19,121 --> 00:27:22,380
The very last set of wheels,

320
00:27:22,381 --> 00:27:25,740
which is at the outer end, is the master.

321
00:27:27,460 --> 00:27:32,460
As that last set of towers or drive unit moves ahead,

322
00:27:32,620 --> 00:27:36,240
it creates an angle difference at a flex joint

323
00:27:36,241 --> 00:27:38,280
above each set of tires.

324
00:27:39,180 --> 00:27:43,980
In the tower box are the switches which sense the angle.

325
00:27:43,981 --> 00:27:48,180
And as the angle changes, a switch is tripped,

326
00:27:48,181 --> 00:27:50,860
which will energize this motor,

327
00:27:50,861 --> 00:27:53,920
and this drive unit will move forward.

328
00:27:53,921 --> 00:27:58,080
It will move until it's back in line again,

329
00:27:58,081 --> 00:27:59,620
and then it will stop.

330
00:27:59,621 --> 00:28:03,120
And this cycle will continue to repeat itself

331
00:28:03,121 --> 00:28:06,060
as the center pivot moves around the field.

332
00:28:07,760 --> 00:28:10,680
Center pivots have revolutionized productivity

333
00:28:10,681 --> 00:28:12,500
by distributing water more efficiently

334
00:28:12,501 --> 00:28:14,240
than any other form of irrigation.

335
00:28:17,260 --> 00:28:19,420
One of the older methods it's eclipsed,

336
00:28:19,421 --> 00:28:21,460
called gravity irrigation

337
00:28:21,461 --> 00:28:26,080
conveys water through furrows dug between rows of crops

338
00:28:26,081 --> 00:28:31,640
it's still used on about sixty percent of america's irrigated cropland

339
00:28:31,641 --> 00:28:35,760
for gravity irrigation the first thing we need is a water source and the

340
00:28:35,761 --> 00:28:39,360
water source we're using here today is out of a local river

341
00:28:39,361 --> 00:28:41,520
what we typically do here is we lower our

342
00:28:41,521 --> 00:28:43,180
basket into the river

343
00:28:43,181 --> 00:28:45,800
and once we have that down into the river

344
00:28:45,801 --> 00:28:55,300
Then we're ready to start this 45 horsepower diesel engine that will deliver the water to our gated pipe at the rate of about 1,200 gallon a minute.

345
00:29:01,320 --> 00:29:05,200
This field is about 1,200 feet long for a quarter of a mile.

346
00:29:05,620 --> 00:29:12,340
We keep the pipe at the top end of the field so that we can run water down the slope to the bottom end to make the gravity system work.

347
00:29:12,341 --> 00:29:19,340
The main advantage or reason that we're using this system here is we have an odd-shaped field that's small,

348
00:29:19,341 --> 00:29:22,340
that's not very conducive to a center pivot system.

349
00:29:22,341 --> 00:29:29,340
Our disadvantages here with this system are we're over-applying water to the extent of about twice as much as we need

350
00:29:29,341 --> 00:29:33,340
on the top end of the field to get water to the bottom end of the field.

351
00:29:35,340 --> 00:29:40,340
The roots of this simple form of irrigation can be traced back 6,000 years

352
00:29:40,341 --> 00:29:43,620
to Mesopotamia in present-day Iraq.

353
00:29:45,620 --> 00:29:47,420
In the millennia that followed,

354
00:29:48,140 --> 00:29:50,120
farmers in arid regions around the globe

355
00:29:50,121 --> 00:29:53,760
have used gravity irrigation to power the progress of their societies.

356
00:29:57,960 --> 00:30:00,680
By the 19th and early 20th centuries,

357
00:30:00,681 --> 00:30:04,020
it was enhancing productivity on America's Great Plains.

358
00:30:05,720 --> 00:30:09,320
But many homesteaders remained at the mercy of the fickle rains.

359
00:30:10,340 --> 00:30:17,340
Just beneath the settlers feet, the vast ocean of the Ogallala beckoned.

360
00:30:17,341 --> 00:30:22,340
But their only means of pumping it to the surface were their underpowered windmills.

361
00:30:22,341 --> 00:30:25,340
They could tap just the shallowest groundwater,

362
00:30:25,341 --> 00:30:31,340
enough only to water a few trees or fill a bathtub on a Saturday night.

363
00:30:31,341 --> 00:30:37,340
The 1920s witnessed unusually generous rains.

364
00:30:37,341 --> 00:30:42,160
But in 1931, the skies abruptly dried up.

365
00:30:46,900 --> 00:30:54,660
The decade-long dust bowl epitomized the grim truth that wherever water vanishes, human misery follows.

366
00:30:58,260 --> 00:31:00,760
Winds whip the dry topsoil away.

367
00:31:02,920 --> 00:31:06,360
And with it, the fortunes and dreams of thousands.

368
00:31:07,340 --> 00:31:21,140
In 1939, the skies finally opened.

369
00:31:21,141 --> 00:31:25,560
But only an effective new system of irrigation could provide farmers the consistent volume

370
00:31:25,561 --> 00:31:32,020
of water they needed so desperately.

371
00:31:32,021 --> 00:31:37,020
In 1948, a Colorado farmer named Frank Zyback finally delivered.

372
00:31:38,020 --> 00:31:42,020
He devised a primitive prototype of the world's first center pivot.

373
00:31:43,020 --> 00:31:45,020
The pipeline was only about knee-high.

374
00:31:45,021 --> 00:31:49,020
The towers were made kind of like a bridge to support with cables.

375
00:31:49,021 --> 00:31:53,020
And the wheels weren't very big and the ground clearance wasn't much.

376
00:31:53,021 --> 00:31:55,020
But he knew he had a good idea.

